      "What Counts as a Security",
      "A state Administrator can only require registration of securities. If something is not a security, the Administrator cannot require it to register. So the first questions are: Is it a security? Is it exempt? Is it sold in an exempt way?\n\nUnder the Uniform Securities Act, a security is an investment that pools money from several investors who expect a profit from someone else's work.\n\nSecurities include:\n• Stocks (common, preferred, treasury stock) and bonds (corporate or government, including debentures)\n• Investment company shares, like mutual funds, closed-end funds, and ETFs\n• Variable annuities and variable life insurance\n• Options, warrants, and rights\n• Interests in oil, gas, or mining ventures and profit-sharing agreements\n• American depositary receipts (ADRs), voting trust certificates, and preorganization certificates\n• Real estate investment trusts (REITs)\n\nNot securities:\n• Fixed annuities and non-variable life insurance, like term, whole, and universal life\n• Commodities, like gold or orange juice, and futures contracts on commodities\n• Precious metals and rare coins\n• Currency\n• Collectibles, like art, antiques, and fine wine\n• Retirement plans themselves, like IRAs and 401(k) plans",
