      "Securities That Skip State Registration",
      "An issuer is a person, usually a company or government, that creates or plans to create a security investors can buy.\n\nSome securities are exempt from state registration because of who issues them:\n• U.S. government and municipal securities, like Treasuries, agency bonds, and municipal bonds\n• Securities of foreign governments the U.S. has diplomatic relations with\n• Securities of banks, savings institutions, trust companies, and credit unions\n• Insurance company securities, like their stocks and bonds, but not variable annuities or variable life\n• Securities of railroads, common carriers, and public utilities regulated by the U.S., a state, or Canada\n• Securities of nonprofits, chambers of commerce, and trade associations\n• Investment-grade commercial paper maturing in nine months or less, in units of at least $50,000\n• Investment contracts tied to employee benefit plans\n\nFederal law also exempts federal covered securities, mainly:\n• Securities listed on a national exchange, like the NYSE or Nasdaq (NYSE Chicago counts too)\n• Bonds or preferred stock of companies with exchange-listed stock\n• Shares of investment companies registered under the Investment Company Act of 1940, like mutual funds\n\nNotice filing is not registration. Mutual funds, UITs, and issuers in federally exempt offerings like Reg D private placements may have to notice file: send their SEC documents, report how much will be sold in the state, consent to service of process, and pay a fee. States cannot require notice filing for exchange-listed securities, and cannot ask for more than federal rules require. An Administrator can issue a stop order on a non-listed federal covered security if the issuer fails to notice file.",
